module Auth.Simple.Token exposing ( Token )

{-| The artifact a successful login hands back: the thing a client presents on
later requests to prove it already authenticated.

This is the third pluggable abstraction in the package, alongside
[`Identifier`](Auth.Simple.Identifier) (who you are) and
[`Credential`](Auth.Simple.Credential) (how you prove it). You combine an
`Identifier` with a `Credential` to obtain a `Token`. A `Token` is the genus; a
database-stored session ([`Auth.Simple.Token.Session`](Auth.Simple.Token.Session))
is its first species, a JWT a future one.

Like the other abstractions, a `Token` is a plain bundle of functions held per
call — it carries no stored configuration. Each species owns its own migrations as
a separate module export (a JWT species may need none), so they are not a field
here.

@docs Token

-}

import Crypto
import Sqlite
import Task exposing ( Task )


{-| A bundle of functions describing one way to issue, verify, and revoke the
token a login hands back.

- `error` — the species' failure type (a stored session uses `Sqlite.Error`).
- `issued` — what `issue` returns, i.e. what `login` hands the caller (a stored
  session returns `{ session, token }`).
- `verified` — what a presented token resolves to on success (a stored session
  resolves to its `Session` row).

`issue` takes only `{ userId }`: lifetime and expiry policy live inside the
implementation, not the caller. `verify` and `revoke` take the raw bearer string
the client holds — uniform across sessions and JWTs.
-}
type alias Token error issued verified =
    { issue : { userId : String } -> Sqlite.Database -> Crypto.SecureContext -> Task error issued
    , verify : String -> Sqlite.Database -> Crypto.SecureContext -> Task error (Maybe verified)
    , revoke : String -> Sqlite.Database -> Crypto.SecureContext -> Task error {}
    }
